SIG — LICENCE POSTURE
=====================

The repository-wide "undetermined" placeholder was resolved by ticket P00.2
(the licensing decision, docs/2_canonical_design_spec.md §42, SIG-LIC-005).
SIG is not a single-licence project: code, data, and documentation carry
different licences, and the data itself is partitioned into mutually
incompatible licence compartments computed per-source at export time
(SIG-LIC-004a). The machine-readable form of this table lives in the `policy/`
package (`policy.licensing`); this file is its human-readable statement.

Resolved licences (SIG-LIC-005 / §42.2)
---------------------------------------

  * Code                     Apache-2.0
      SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
      Patent grant and trademark reservation. AGPL was rejected: the valuable
      asset is the graph, not the crawler, and AGPL would deter adoption by
      exactly the institutions Goal 8 targets. Every first-party source file
      carries the `Apache-2.0` SPDX tag.

  * SIG-original graph data   CC-BY-4.0
      Attribution keeps the provenance chain alive downstream — SIG's whole
      thesis. CC0 was rejected for that reason.

  * OSM-derived physical assets   ODbL-1.0   (separate table and export file)
      Share-alike; published as a physically separate compartment (§42.3,
      Strategy B). Not mergeable into the CC-BY-4.0 graph.

  * Portal layer (Eyes on Flock–derived)   CC-BY-SA-4.0   (separate compartment)
      Share-alike; not mergeable with ODbL, and not foldable into CC-BY-4.0.

  * Documentation             CC-BY-4.0
  * Ontology and vocabularies CC0-1.0
      A vocabulary succeeds only by adoption; every obligation is an adoption
      tax, and term lists are barely copyrightable.

  * SIG data contributed upstream to OpenStreetMap   CC0-1.0   (dual-licensed
      for that purpose only, SIG-LIC-007a) — satisfies OSM's no-additional-
      copyright import rule; scoped to the contributed subset, not the graph.

The full text of Apache-2.0 governs the code in this repository. The referenced
data/documentation licences are the standard published texts of ODbL-1.0,
CC-BY-4.0, CC-BY-SA-4.0, and CC0-1.0 respectively.

Per-source and export-time rights
----------------------------------

Every source and evidence artifact carries a rights record (SIG-LIC-001): an
SPDX expression (using `LicenseRef-SIG-<slug>` for bespoke terms), an attribution
string, a **separately reviewed** `redistributable` boolean (never inferred from
the licence string, SIG-LIC-003), derivative permission, an `ai_training_permitted`
grant (SIG-LIC-004b), the terms URL, and the retrieval date. A source with
unresolved rights is `UNDETERMINED` and fails the export gate closed
(SIG-LIC-004). Export licences are computed from constituent rights and the build
fails on incompatibility (SIG-LIC-010); the compatibility gate runs per
compartment (see `policy.licensing`).

Items referred to counsel before launch (SIG-LIC-009) are listed in the risk
register (docs/risk_register.md).
